course: dictionary
lesson: 15214
title: Words about the senses
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sensory is an adjective. It means to do with the senses: sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell. Look for the word sense inside it, and you have a clue. «The sensory garden had soft grass, sweet herbs and a trickling water tray.» The garden is made for the senses, so you can see, smell, touch and hear it.
explain: Sensory can also describe a special room, toy or garden. These are made to let people enjoy or calm themselves through the senses. «Our sensory room has dim lights and gentle music to help us relax.» The lights and music are there to make the room calm, so it is a sensory room.
explain: Your body has sensory organs: eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin. They collect information, and sensory nerves carry it to the brain. When a place has too much noise, light or touch at once, that is called sensory overload.
fact: Sensory means to do with the senses.
fact: The five senses are sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell.
fact: Sensory is built from the word sense, so think of your senses when you see it.
fact: Sensory organs (eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin) collect information for the brain.
fact: «Sensory overload» means too much noise, light or touch at once.
exercise: «The sensory garden had soft grass, sweet herbs and a trickling water tray.» What does sensory mean here? | To do with school sports | To do with money | *To do with the senses | To do with sleeping
exercise: Which of these is a sensory organ? | Your ankles | *Your ears | Your elbows | Your knees
exercise: Which of these is one of the five senses? | Thinking | *Smell | Sleeping | Memory
test: «Our sensory room has dim lights and gentle music to help us relax.» What is a sensory room for? | To store old school books | To play football indoors | To hold a maths test | *To help people enjoy or calm themselves through the senses
test: «The sensory toy crinkled and squeaked when Mia squeezed it.» Which senses does this toy use? | *Hearing and touch | Taste only | Smell only | Sleep and dreams
exam: Which phrase means too much noise, light or touch at once? | *Sensory overload | Sensory room | Sensory play | Sensory garden
